<h3>System details — what's inside, and in what order to read</h3>

<h4>Knowledge System — this wiki explained (6 docs)</h4>
<p>A self-describing System that explains, with real data, how this wiki's knowledge accumulates, connects, and gets read: <a href="#!kgs-overview">How Knowledge Accumulates</a> (4 tiers · registration pipeline) → <a href="#!kgs-edges">How Documents Connect</a> (concept curation · idf edges · directional typed relations) → <a href="#!kgs-views">Nine Views of the Graph</a> (which view answers which question · hubs · bridges) → <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">How AI Reads This Wiki</a> (machine-readable layer) → <a href="#!kgs-plugin">Packaging the Wiki Engine as a Plugin</a> (wiki-plugin) → <a href="#!kgs-gates">How the Wiki Guards Itself</a> (gates &amp; automation). Read in order to see where every number on this map comes from.</p>

<h4>Reflections — this wiki seen from outside (1 doc)</h4>
<p>Where Knowledge System explains how this wiki works, Reflections asks <strong>where it stands compared with other approaches</strong>. <a href="#!wr-knowledge-vault">The Man Who Records Everything, the Wiki That Publishes</a> puts a lifelogger's system — a necklace recorder and screen capture taking in a whole life, privately — next to this wiki, and shows the two sitting at opposite ends of a diagonal on the axes of capture, openness, discipline, and emotional payoff. The conclusion is not "record more" but to borrow two things: keeping the reason for capture as data, and putting retrospectives on a cycle.</p>

<h4>CLAUDE.md — the house rules you hand an AI (1 doc)</h4>
<p>A single doc, <a href="#!claude-md-commandments">The Ten Commandments of CLAUDE.md</a>, on what belongs in a repository's rules file when an AI pair programmer reads it. This wiki's own <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is the working example, so reading it beside <a href="#!kgs-gates">How the Wiki Guards Itself</a> shows how written promises and machine enforcement interlock.</p>

<h4>Introduction — first steps with Claude Code (2 docs)</h4>
<p>The entry System: what Claude Code is and how to get started. <a href="#!welcome">Getting Started</a> covers the tool itself, installation and auth, slash commands, CLAUDE.md, and plan mode in one piece, and <a href="#!skills">the extending chapter</a> introduces the three extension axes — Skills, MCP Servers, and Hooks. Its concepts (install, slash commands, CLAUDE.md) carry straight into the Tutorial and OMC Systems.</p>

<h4>Tutorial (Basics) — the foundation course (4 docs)</h4>
<p>The documented basics course. <a href="#!ccb-overview">Basics</a> gets you through the gate with the overview, terminal, and CLAUDE.md; <a href="#!ccb-research-web">Practice</a> runs research, design-system extraction, and paper digests in one flow; <a href="#!ccb-skills">Extending</a> covers skills, plugins, and agents; and <a href="#!ccb-manage">Operations</a> closes with usage, context, and session management.</p>

<h4>Skill — skills deep dive (3 docs)</h4>
<p>A first-principles System on why and how skills work. <a href="#!skl-overview">What skills are</a> sets the coding-agent background and the strengths; <a href="#!skl-context">Structure &amp; mechanics</a> covers the context window, progressive loading, SKILL.md and examples; <a href="#!skl-install">Install · security · creating</a> goes from hands-on use to skill-creator — its closing engineering-layers wrap relates skills to MCP and harnesses, bridging to the automation branch.</p>

<h4>Second Brain — five levels of knowledge management (4 docs)</h4>
<p>The System that explains personal and organizational knowledge management as a level model, from <strong>level 1 (find precisely) to level 5 (an autonomous brain OS)</strong>. <a href="#!sb-intro">What a second brain is</a> sets the concept and the five-level terrain at a glance; <a href="#!sb-level1">Levels 1–2</a> and <a href="#!sb-level3">Levels 3–5</a> climb the floors; <a href="#!sb-context-connections">Applying it</a> helps decide what to put in, which level to choose, and how to extend to a team. The LLM Wiki System is the concrete embodiment of this model's level 2.</p>

<h4>LLM Wiki — an Obsidian-based knowledge vault (3 docs)</h4>
<p>The methodology System for running an LLM-friendly wiki on Obsidian vaults. <a href="#!llmw-overview">The System &amp; Capture</a> sets up the two-vault architecture and the capture habit; <a href="#!llmw-metadata">Note Anatomy</a> dissects metadata, links, wiki cards, and rules; <a href="#!llmw-onboard">Practice &amp; Philosophy</a> closes with onboarding, Ingest · Query · Lint, the web clipper, and the philosophy of running two vaults.</p>

<h4>Harness Engineering — the agent-team methodology (4 docs)</h4>
<p>The agent-team methodology System. <a href="#!hns-overview">What a harness is</a> sets the definition, anatomy, and impact; <a href="#!hns-install">Running it</a> covers the install–run–review practice; <a href="#!hns-kitchen">How it works</a> unpacks the kitchen metaphor, agent teams, and the core three; <a href="#!hns-usecases">Uses and extensions</a> covers cases, Harness 100, and tool combinations.</p>

<h4>Harness Plugin — harnesses for real ERP work (4 docs)</h4>
<p>The plugin System that applies the harness methodology to <strong>real ERP customization work</strong>. After <a href="#!hnp-intro">Intro · Concepts · Install</a>, practice immediately with <a href="#!hnp-first-run">The First Run and How It Works</a>; <a href="#!hnp-agents-skills">Design and Verification</a> covers agent and skill design with _workspace quality control, and <a href="#!hnp-scenarios">Scenarios · Harness 100 · Commands</a> covers usage. The theory of the Harness Engineering System meets the business domain here.</p>

<h4>OMC Plugin — execution modes and autonomy (5 docs)</h4>
<p>The usage System for the oh-my-claudecode plugin. <a href="#!omc-intro">Introduction &amp; Install</a> sets up the environment with terms, install, and diagnosis; <a href="#!omc-modes">Execution Modes</a> (autopilot · ralph · ultrawork · team) covers the core feature; <a href="#!omc-agents">Agents · Skills and Hooks · Memory</a> covers the building blocks. <a href="#!omc-tools">Advanced Use</a> (tools, workflows, Windows) and <a href="#!omc-cost">Cost, Performance, and the Command Reference</a> are the operations chapters.</p>

<h4>Skills — one skill at a time (1 doc)</h4>
<p>Where the Skill System above teaches how skills work, this one <strong>introduces individual skills</strong>. So far that is <a href="#!sks-im-not-ai">im-not-ai (Humanize KR)</a>, an editing skill that catches 33 patterns that give AI-written prose away. It carries the record of running that skill back over 121 docs of this wiki, so you can see what it actually filters out rather than what it claims to.</p>

<h4>Tip — Claude Code tips (3 docs)</h4>
<p>A practical collection of 28 Claude Code tips grounded in the official docs, arranged in three parts. <a href="#!tip-beginner">Beginner</a> covers nine context-saving habits (/init, /context, /compact vs /clear, plan mode), <a href="#!tip-intermediate">Intermediate</a> covers eleven ways to turn sessions into a system (parallel subagents, skills, CLAUDE.md as a router, /rewind, hooks), and <a href="#!tip-advanced">Advanced</a> covers eight tips for parallel, autonomous, scheduled work (worktrees, permission editing, agent teams). Unlike the course Systems, read it like a dictionary — pick only the tips you need.</p>

<h4>GraphDB — databases that store relationships (4 docs)</h4>
<p>Databases that treat <strong>the relationships between entities</strong> as first-class citizens instead of rows and columns. The sidebar lists these by registration date, but a newcomer is better off starting with <a href="#!gdb-graphrag">Graph Databases &amp; GraphRAG</a> (fish with vectors, walk with graphs) and then building one by hand in <a href="#!gdb-vector-retriever">Your First Vector Retriever</a> on the Neo4j sandbox. Model choice comes next: <a href="#!news-20260711-neo4j-rdf-vs-property-graph">RDF or Property Graph</a> compares the two lineages, and <a href="#!news-20260710-oracle-ontology-property-graph">Oracle Ontology &amp; Property Graph</a> shows a vendor doing the same work on top of an existing relational database.</p>

<h4>Ontology — the layer that defines meaning (2 docs)</h4>
<p>If GraphDB is storage technology, an ontology is the layer above it that defines <strong>what means what, and which rules apply</strong>. <a href="#!gdb-onto-concepts">What Is an Ontology</a> sorts out the concepts, the semantic layer, and hallucination reduction through the warehouse-versus-map analogy, and <a href="#!gdb-onto-adoption">How Companies Adopt Ontologies</a> picks up the practice with industry cases, ROI, the four build stages, and governance. Both mark vendor-supplied figures as such, so read them with the fact-check notes.</p>

<h4>News &amp; Articles — what got read along the way (11 docs)</h4>
<p>Write-ups of model launches, papers, and talks. Unlike the course Systems, <strong>there is no order</strong> — start with whatever catches your eye. On models there are <a href="#!news-20260724-claude-opus-5">Claude Opus 5</a>, <a href="#!news-20260716-kimi-k3">Kimi K3</a>, and <a href="#!news-20260801-astra-math">OpenAI Astra</a>; on how the work changes, <a href="#!news-20260728-software-factories-fail">Harness Engineering Is Not Enough</a>, <a href="#!news-20260803-cherny-karpathy">The One Who Deleted and the One Who Floated</a>, <a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">Defaults That Stop Asking</a>, and <a href="#!news-20260724-snucatch-ai-coding">The End of Coding by Hand?</a> answer the same question from different angles. <a href="#!news-20260709-ai-2040-plan-a">AI 2040: Plan A</a> used to be a four-part sub-folder but is now merged into a single article, sitting alongside the other pieces.</p>

